00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:43.797
- And you would not believe
how many sheriffs told me,

00:01:43.797 --> 00:01:45.407
"Oh yeah, just down the street,

00:01:45.407 --> 00:01:46.970
"there's a guy that has a lion"

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.410
or a guy down there that has a tiger.

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:51.810
They just feel very
strongly that these are mine

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.650
and nobody's gonna take 'em from me.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.010
- Okay, so right off the bat,

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:58.630
let's talk about the
legality of owning big cats

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:00.760
because a ton of people
reached out to me and asked

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:03.680
how it was even possible
that these crazy people

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:06.610
could own these lions and tigers.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:09.220
Well, the reason is because
there's no federal law

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:12.940
that actually bans the
private ownership of big cats.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:14.850
There is a patchwork of state laws

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:18.250
that some ban outright
ownership of big cats.

00:02:18.250 --> 00:02:21.240
Some require regulations
for owning big cats

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:24.260
but there's no federal law that
says you can't own big cats.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:25.890
So whether you personally (chuckles)

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.630
could own a big cat like Joe Exotic

00:02:28.630 --> 00:02:31.110
depends largely on what
state you live in and whether

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.580
that state prohibits the
ownership of wildlife.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.360
Now there are some federal laws

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.660
that do provide some
regulation in this context

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:38.960
like the Endangered Species Act,

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.140
which prohibits the sale of big cats

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:44.500
without a permit from U.S.
Fish and Wildlife Department

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.670
and in 2016 there was actually a case

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:48.940
that applied the Endangered Species Act

00:02:48.940 --> 00:02:51.200
not only to animals that
are found in the wild

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.660
but also to animals that
are held in captivity,

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.030
so that's a relatively
new part of this law.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:57.780
And then there's the Animal Welfare Act,

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.490
which sets certain minimal standards

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:03.500
for actually caring for a lot
of these animals and big cats

00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.020
and requires you to get a permit

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:06.920
to exhibit these things to the public.

00:03:06.920 --> 00:03:09.100
And, interestingly, Tim Stark,

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.480
who is featured later
on in this documentary,

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.520
had his permit permanently revoked

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:14.970
by the federal government,

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:18.260
so he can't exhibit animals
to the public anymore.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:21.120
And it probably won't
surprise you that Oklahoma

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.340
has some of the laxest
laws in the United States

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:26.490
when it comes to owning exotic animals,

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.550
which is why Joe Exotic was able

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.055
to have his zoo in Oklahoma.

00:03:31.055 --> 00:03:32.380
(gentle music)

00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:34.160
- There's always been a rumor out there

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:37.970
that Doc Antle euthanizes his
cubs when he's done with them.

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:40.980
- [Carol] We've had
whistle blowers come to us

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.800
and say that they've heard
a gunshot in the middle

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.050
of the night and the
next day there's no cat.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.550
But they don't know where the cat went.

00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:50.820
- Yeah, so there are
all kinds of allegations

00:03:50.820 --> 00:03:53.240
in this documentary,
not just that Joe Exotic

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:56.550
but other zoo owners
would kill the big cats

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.440
once they were no longer young enough

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:00.500
to be handled by the general public.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:02.876
You can imagine that once
they get to be a certain age,

00:04:02.876 --> 00:04:05.660
(laughs lightly) they become
ferocious killing machines

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:07.570
and are no longer cuddly animals

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:09.050
that you can hold in your hands.

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:12.400
And so once they lose
their economic value,

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.010
effectively these zoo
owners would euthanize them

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:17.660
and, under most
circumstances, it is illegal

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:21.070
to kill big cats under
the Animal Welfare Act.

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:23.310
So, spoiler alert, one of the things

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:26.600
that Joe is eventually
convicted of is a violation

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.640
of this act because he's accused

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.188
of killing a bunch of
tigers without a permit.

00:04:31.188 --> 00:04:34.110
(gentle music)
- They said that Joe had shot

00:04:34.110 --> 00:04:39.000
five tigers and they dug
'em up and found the skulls.

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.200
- So there's a big
difference between being able

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.050
to actually legally own
one of these animals

00:04:44.050 --> 00:04:46.030
and being able to take care of it

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:47.540
or being able to euthanize it.

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:49.663
Those are very separate questions.

00:04:49.663 --> 00:04:50.496
(gentle music)
- And I told Joe,

00:04:50.496 --> 00:04:51.329
"How are you gonna make money?

00:04:51.329 --> 00:04:52.570
"How you gonna make money?
How you gonna make money?"

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:54.400
He didn't have any other way to make money

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:56.385
other than cub pettin',
so he had to breed.

00:04:56.385 --> 00:04:57.218
(crowd oohs and ahhs)
- Oh my God.

00:04:57.218 --> 00:04:58.473
- They're only a hour old.

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:02.240
- This is another question
that kept cropping up

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.602
in terms of whether you're
allowed to breed these big cats.

00:05:05.602 --> 00:05:09.480
And under federal law, as long
as you have a USDA license,

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.330
you can in fact breed big cats,

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:15.130
though some states in
particular have banned

00:05:15.130 --> 00:05:19.070
breeding for obvious reasons
because it's ripe for abuse.

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:21.100
So, again, there's a
difference between federal law

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.560
and state law in terms of whether

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:23.760
you're allowed to breed them.

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.210
There's no federal law
that says you can't,

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.060
as long as you have a permit

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:30.597
but a lot of states do
in fact prohibit that.

00:05:30.597 --> 00:05:31.732
(suspenseful music)
(dial tone)

00:05:31.732 --> 00:05:32.916
(phone numbers beeping)

00:05:32.916 --> 00:05:34.808
- [Dispatcher] 911, what's your emergency?

00:05:34.808 --> 00:05:35.883
- [Woman] We've got an
employee that was attacked

00:05:35.883 --> 00:05:38.950
by a tiger and he's hurt bad.

00:05:38.950 --> 00:05:42.199
- The arm is completely gone,
we do not have time to wait.

00:05:42.199 --> 00:05:43.370
(animal squawking)

00:05:43.370 --> 00:05:44.700
So get your gurney here.

00:05:45.965 --> 00:05:48.831
Get everything out of the driveway.

00:05:48.831 --> 00:05:50.031
- [Man] Is he all right?

00:05:50.887 --> 00:05:52.630
- (scoffs) This is such a sad affair,

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:55.650
where the employee was
so grievously injured.

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:58.320
In terms of whether the zoo is liable,

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:01.330
states again differ in terms
of how they impose liability

00:06:01.330 --> 00:06:05.230
for injuries that are caused
by these exotic animals.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:08.860
Some states hold the zoos
what' called strictly liable,

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:11.300
which means that, as long
as as there is an injury

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:15.040
related to the animals,
the zoo is responsible

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.400
regardless of whether they
were negligent or not.

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:22.310
Other states require that the
zoos be actually negligent

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:26.280
in terms of an employee
being injured or not.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.730
You could imagine that
(chuckles), based on some

00:06:28.730 --> 00:06:30.670
of the the things that
are in this documentary,

00:06:30.670 --> 00:06:34.180
it probably wouldn't be
too hard to show that Joe

00:06:34.180 --> 00:06:36.010
and some of the other staff at the zoo

00:06:36.010 --> 00:06:37.650
might have been negligent in terms

00:06:37.650 --> 00:06:40.350
of allowing this person to be injured.

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:42.515
- I've been doing this 20
years, never even been bit.

00:06:42.515 --> 00:06:43.998
(tiger growls)

00:06:43.998 --> 00:06:45.180
(chain link rattles)
(man grunts) It's okay.

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:46.600
- But we don't have all of the facts

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:47.490
in this particular case.

00:06:47.490 --> 00:06:49.060
I think this documentary made it a point

00:06:49.060 --> 00:06:50.690
not to show too much of the footage

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:53.490
related to this employee being injured.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:56.020
There might be defenses the zoo might have

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.080
like contributory negligence
or assumption of risk

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.100
where perhaps the employee
might not have been

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.380
exercising all of the caution
that she should've been using.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:06.680
So it's not necessarily
an open and shut case

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:09.270
and just because you have a legal claim

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:11.270
or a lawsuit on your hands
doesn't necessarily mean

00:07:11.270 --> 00:07:12.460
that you're gonna recover anything.

00:07:12.460 --> 00:07:15.100
There've been so many
lawsuits against Joe Exotic

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:17.870
and the G.W. Zoo that
it's entirely possible

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:20.160
that even if this employee won the lawsuit

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:21.930
that she might not be
able to recover anything.

00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:23.720
That's called being judgment proof

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:25.770
and it's, unfortunately, a sad reality

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.280
where sometimes the guilty party

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:30.340
just doesn't have any funds to compensate

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:32.143
an injury for someone that deserves it.

00:07:32.143 --> 00:07:33.170
(gentle music)

00:07:33.170 --> 00:07:34.400
- This is probably gonna get me in trouble

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:38.195
but we used to eat off the meat truck.

00:07:38.195 --> 00:07:40.280
(truck beeping)

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:42.342
- It was a truck that picked up meat

00:07:42.342 --> 00:07:43.190
(Devin laughs lightly)
from all these Walmarts

00:07:43.190 --> 00:07:44.780
in the area that had expired.

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:47.270
Joe would let them pick
out what they wanted first

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:50.940
and they'd carry bags of
this expired meat and foods

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:54.180
back to their mobile home
and that's what they ate.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:57.610
- Oh my God, this violates so
many Health and Safety codes

00:07:57.610 --> 00:07:59.300
and OSHA in so many different ways,

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:01.310
I'm not even gonna go into it.

00:08:01.310 --> 00:08:04.260
This is actually worse than
Willy Wonka's sweatshop factory,

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:06.990
which I covered in an
episode of "Laws Broken",

00:08:06.990 --> 00:08:08.697
so if you wanna know how many laws

00:08:08.697 --> 00:08:11.150
"Willy Wonka and the
Chocolate Factory" broke,

00:08:11.150 --> 00:08:12.510
you can check out that episode.

00:08:12.510 --> 00:08:15.198
Spoiler alert, it's a lot of laws.

00:08:15.198 --> 00:08:16.750
(suspenseful music)
- I was the power of attorney

00:08:16.750 --> 00:08:17.733
for both of them.

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:20.560
- [Interviewer] Who was
the power of attorney

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:22.573
on the new documents Carol produced?

00:08:24.030 --> 00:08:24.863
- Not me.

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:31.480
That new power of attorney gave
her control over the estate.

00:08:31.480 --> 00:08:33.510
- Ah, the will and the power of attorney

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:36.380
being changed in a very suspicious way,

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:39.790
right before Carol's husband goes missing.

00:08:39.790 --> 00:08:43.590
At first blush, this I
think looks pretty terrible

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:46.130
but I don't think that
this is nearly as bad

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:49.210
as it appears and hear me out on this.

00:08:49.210 --> 00:08:51.150
As it happens, you cannot execute

00:08:51.150 --> 00:08:54.010
a durable power of attorney
without two witnesses

00:08:54.010 --> 00:08:56.830
and a notary and the
notary will actually record

00:08:56.830 --> 00:09:00.620
that they witnessed the
signatures being provided.

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:03.130
Now that law actually changed in 2011,

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:06.450
where now under Florida durable

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:08.470
and nondurable powers of attorney

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:11.920
have to be with two
witnesses and a notary.

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:13.870
Before that, only durable
powers of attorney.

00:09:13.870 --> 00:09:17.270
Meaning that a power of
attorney that applies

00:09:17.270 --> 00:09:20.590
when someone is incapacitated
or goes missing, for example.

00:09:20.590 --> 00:09:22.980
So I think the law at the time required

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:24.270
two witnesses and a notary,

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:26.450
which means it's unlikely
that there was any

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:29.030
foul play involved and her husband

00:09:29.030 --> 00:09:30.640
would have had to have signed off

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:33.370
on this particular power of attorney.

00:09:33.370 --> 00:09:36.670
So it probably was not
executed posthumously.

00:09:36.670 --> 00:09:39.070
Now, on top of that,
there is this potentially

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:41.330
suspicious language about kicking in

00:09:41.330 --> 00:09:43.240
when the person disappears,

00:09:43.240 --> 00:09:45.930
that doesn't seem like
something that often happens.

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:46.990
- [Interviewer] Isn't it suspicious

00:09:46.990 --> 00:09:50.630
that the power of attorney
says upon my disappearance?

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:52.620
Is that normal verbiage?

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:56.100
- I have, in 37 years, never seen it say

00:09:56.100 --> 00:09:59.330
or disappearance, never have.

00:09:59.330 --> 00:10:00.970
- I actually did a little bit of research

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:03.610
to find out if this
disappearance language is normal.

00:10:03.610 --> 00:10:06.780
And, as it happens, I was able to find

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:09.480
another power of attorney that mentions

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.020
disappearance, which makes sense.

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:14.360
If someone is incapacitated,
if they go into a coma,

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:16.460
if they disappear, if they are killed,

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:18.970
that's when you want a
durable power of attorney,

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:21.200
for someone to be able to take care

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:22.850
of the things and the estate.

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:24.540
The documentary seems to indicate

00:10:24.540 --> 00:10:26.930
that it's only applicable
in disappearance.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:30.000
I think that they sort of
cherry picked this one section.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:32.740
I would bet that the rest
of the power of attorney

00:10:32.740 --> 00:10:34.840
mentions a whole bunch
of different situations

00:10:34.840 --> 00:10:36.220
where the power of attorney kicks in.

00:10:36.220 --> 00:10:38.830
So I could be wrong, I
don't have all of the facts,

00:10:38.830 --> 00:10:41.140
but I don't think this
is nearly as suspicious

00:10:41.140 --> 00:10:43.340
as it's portrayed in the documentary.

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:46.450
- I got a phone call stating
that the office alarm

00:10:46.450 --> 00:10:49.460
had gone off and Carol was there.

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:54.053
Her and Kenny Farr had
cut the locks on the gate,

00:10:54.053 --> 00:10:54.886
(suspenseful music)

00:10:54.886 --> 00:10:57.350
cut the locks on the office.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:10:58.700
The will and the power of attorney,

00:10:58.700 --> 00:11:01.700
they were all taken out
of the office that day.

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:03.250
- So we talked about the potentially

00:11:03.250 --> 00:11:05.570
suspicious language of
the power of attorney.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:08.750
Now a lot of those accusations
came from Anne McQueen,

00:11:08.750 --> 00:11:13.530
Don's old assistant, who talks
about a lot of these things.

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:16.280
And certainly casts
aspersions on Carol Baskin.

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:19.980
Interestingly, there was
a conservatorship case

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:23.840
for Don's estate that accuses Anne McQueen

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:27.860
of embezzling $600,000
from Don's properties.

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:29.780
If indeed these allegations are true,

00:11:29.780 --> 00:11:32.880
that certainly paints her aspersions

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:34.810
in a different light and would explain

00:11:34.810 --> 00:11:37.620
why Carol did not want Anne accessing

00:11:37.620 --> 00:11:40.620
all of the documents that
were in Don's office.

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:42.320
But, this is Florida, so I assume some

00:11:42.320 --> 00:11:44.890
light embezzlement is probably okay.

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:49.730
- Joe decided that the way
he would get back at us

00:11:49.730 --> 00:11:53.170
was to rename his traveling show

00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:55.004
Big Cat Rescue Entertainment.

00:11:55.004 --> 00:11:57.200
(suspenseful music)
Here's our logo, all right,

00:11:57.200 --> 00:12:00.160
and here is what he started using.

00:12:00.160 --> 00:12:03.000
- All right, now we get
into the real sexy stuff,

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.680
the trademark infringement cases.

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:07.970
So I had to look into this.

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:10.170
There were federal lawsuits filed.

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:11.330
There were some trademark cases

00:12:11.330 --> 00:12:13.070
and there were some copyright cases.

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:16.490
And, from what I can tell, the
trademark infringement suit

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:19.520
is basically as it's
portrayed in the documentary.

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:22.720
Carol Baskin had the
trademark to Big Cat Rescue

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:25.300
for a very long time and then it appears

00:12:25.300 --> 00:12:28.770
that Joe Exotic wanted to
(chuckles) create confusion

00:12:28.770 --> 00:12:32.800
between Big Cat Rescue and
his own entertainment company.

00:12:32.800 --> 00:12:34.500
- Joe started realizing
if he made his name

00:12:34.500 --> 00:12:35.890
close to the Big Cat Rescue,

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:38.300
when they google it, it
might pull him up first.

00:12:38.300 --> 00:12:39.400
- You're not allowed to do that.

00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:41.020
That's the entire point of trademark.

00:12:41.020 --> 00:12:44.670
Trademark exists to
prevent consumer confusion

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:47.560
and trademark is not
necessarily the graphics,

00:12:47.560 --> 00:12:49.760
although it can include the graphics,

00:12:49.760 --> 00:12:52.650
but it also includes
just the wordmark itself.

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:56.479
So Big Cat Rescue versus Big
Cat Rescue Entertainment,

00:12:56.479 --> 00:12:59.590
that's gonna be a slam dunk trademark case

00:12:59.590 --> 00:13:02.810
for trademark infringement,
false designation of origin

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:04.310
and unfair competition.

00:13:04.310 --> 00:13:06.260
It's not surprising then that Carol

00:13:06.260 --> 00:13:09.347
got a judgment for close
to a million dollars,

00:13:09.347 --> 00:13:14.347
$953,000 because Joe couldn't help himself

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:16.990
and he probably admitted
that he tried (chuckles)

00:13:16.990 --> 00:13:19.987
to create confusion
between the two entities.

00:13:19.987 --> 00:13:20.820
(gentle music)
- The one lawsuit

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:22.250
was about the rabbit photo

00:13:22.250 --> 00:13:24.400
and the other lawsuit was
about a bunch of photos

00:13:24.400 --> 00:13:29.190
where he would take a picture
of Carol and take her head

00:13:29.190 --> 00:13:33.650
and put it on the body
of a man in a diaper.

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:36.610
- So these copyright cases
and there are at least

00:13:36.610 --> 00:13:39.010
two of them, possibly
three copyright cases,

00:13:39.010 --> 00:13:41.490
are a little different
from the trademark case

00:13:41.490 --> 00:13:42.630
that we just talked about.

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:45.020
So this may or may not be meritorious

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:49.120
but Joe, obviously, would use graphics

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:53.530
in his reality TV show
and his internet videos

00:13:53.530 --> 00:13:57.700
and he may not have been
using them in a fair use way.

00:13:57.700 --> 00:13:59.330
- Good afternoon, ladies and gentleman.

00:13:59.330 --> 00:14:01.380
I'm Joe Exotic and we're gonna make

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:03.137
an honest woman out of this (beeps).

00:14:03.137 --> 00:14:08.137
- And what Carol did is sort
of diabolical and genius

00:14:08.210 --> 00:14:11.230
because what she did is
she went and tracked down

00:14:11.230 --> 00:14:13.630
the people that took the
photos that Joe used.

00:14:13.630 --> 00:14:16.410
So maybe they were posted on
Facebook or on the internet

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:18.150
and she would track down the people

00:14:18.150 --> 00:14:21.000
who took the photos of Big
Cat Rescue or her husband

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:25.330
or herself and she would buy
the rights to those photos.

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.500
She would get what's called
a copyright assignment

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:31.610
and then, with the ownership
of the copyright procured,

00:14:31.610 --> 00:14:34.500
she then sued Joe for
copyright infringement

00:14:34.500 --> 00:14:38.260
for using the photos that
then she had the rights to.

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:41.170
Carol Baskin, credit where
credit is due, it's clever.

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:42.003
(gentle music)
- She'll never get

00:14:42.003 --> 00:14:44.777
a million dollars out of
me, I don't own anything.

00:14:45.884 --> 00:14:48.270
I don't own anything.

00:14:48.270 --> 00:14:52.270
This bus is not mine,
those cars are not mine.

00:14:52.270 --> 00:14:54.333
(chuckles) I'm not stupid.

00:14:55.583 --> 00:14:57.040
(laughs)

00:14:57.040 --> 00:15:00.890
- Okay, this may be my favorite
part of all of "Tiger King".

00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:03.530
You can keep the conspiracy
to commit murder,

00:15:03.530 --> 00:15:05.590
you can keep the tigers and lions,

00:15:05.590 --> 00:15:08.570
my favorite thing are
the fraudulent transfers.

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:10.240
- Joe kept thinking if
he changed the name,

00:15:10.240 --> 00:15:11.820
it would just derail him and derail him,

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:14.270
well it didn't, they just
moved it to another lawsuit.

00:15:14.270 --> 00:15:19.170
- Contrary to what Joe and Jeff Lowe say,

00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:22.980
you can't avoid judgments
or avoid lawsuits

00:15:22.980 --> 00:15:25.320
just by changing the name of the company

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:29.200
or just taking the assets and
moving them somewhere else.

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:31.380
That's called a fraudulent transfer.

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:33.880
It creates what's called
a constructive trust

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:35.710
over the transferred assets.

00:15:35.710 --> 00:15:38.750
So if you take possession of those assets

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:40.789
that were fraudulently transferred,

00:15:40.789 --> 00:15:45.330
the person who is rightfully
owning that property,

00:15:45.330 --> 00:15:48.280
which as we saw, Carol got a judgment

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:49.440
for almost a million dollars,

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:52.510
so she could've gotten almost everything

00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:54.570
that the G.W. Zoo owned,

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:58.990
that person can then claw
back all of that property

00:15:58.990 --> 00:16:00.840
that was transferred somewhere else.

00:16:00.840 --> 00:16:05.150
So what I think happened here
is that Joe Exotic's mom,

00:16:05.150 --> 00:16:08.570
Shirley Schreibvogel, wasn't sued per se

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:11.160
but what it looks like is
that Joe put some documents

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:14.150
in front of her where it, on paper,

00:16:14.150 --> 00:16:16.680
transferred the assets of the zoo itself

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:21.054
or the things that zoo owned
and put it in her name.

00:16:21.054 --> 00:16:21.887
(suspenseful music)
- I went down to the

00:16:21.887 --> 00:16:23.980
courthouse and got a
copy of one of the deeds

00:16:23.980 --> 00:16:27.350
and a I said, "Grandma, are
you aware that this happened?"

00:16:27.350 --> 00:16:29.187
And she said, "Well, he
shoved a paper in front of me

00:16:29.187 --> 00:16:31.660
"and told me to sign it and I
didn't think anything of it."

00:16:31.660 --> 00:16:34.130
- Obviously, when they
signed these documents

00:16:34.130 --> 00:16:37.760
and engaged in the,
knowingly or unknowingly,

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:39.960
in a fraudulent transfer with Joe,

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:42.960
their involvement almost
certainly required a lawyer

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.570
and it really seemed like
she rubber stamped things

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:48.636
that she really shouldn't
have been rubber stamping.

00:16:48.636 --> 00:16:49.469
(gentle music)
- Shirley's name

00:16:49.469 --> 00:16:51.760
is on everything, the
trailers, the water bill,

00:16:51.760 --> 00:16:55.440
the electric bill, the cable bills.

00:16:55.440 --> 00:16:56.890
- Yeah, you can't get away with that.

00:16:56.890 --> 00:17:01.890
And I'm sorry that Joe
seemingly took advantage of her

00:17:02.060 --> 00:17:05.450
but Shirley Schreibvogel did
sign a settlement agreement

00:17:05.450 --> 00:17:07.770
with Big Cat Rescue Corp, which is the one

00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:11.770
owned by Carol Baskin, not
the fake one owned by Joe,

00:17:11.770 --> 00:17:16.630
where she agreed that she had
engaged in these transfers.

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:19.520
So let that be a lesson to
all those parents out there,

00:17:19.520 --> 00:17:22.190
don't sign paperwork
just because your kids

00:17:22.190 --> 00:17:23.350
put it in front of you.

00:17:23.350 --> 00:17:25.120
They might be engaging in fraud,

00:17:25.120 --> 00:17:27.400
especially if they live in Florida.

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:29.453
(suspenseful music)
- One time I was on the park

00:17:29.453 --> 00:17:30.817
and Joe's talking to me, he says,

00:17:30.817 --> 00:17:32.387
"Hey, I hear you can take care

00:17:32.387 --> 00:17:34.770
"of a problem for me in Florida."

00:17:34.770 --> 00:17:35.670
I said, "What problem would that be?"

00:17:35.670 --> 00:17:36.920
He says, "Carol Baskin."

00:17:36.920 --> 00:17:38.970
I said, "No, I can't."

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:40.200
- All right, now we're getting into

00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:43.670
the real juicy stuff here, the
conspiracy to commit murder.

00:17:43.670 --> 00:17:46.500
Now, you might be wondering,
when does idle chitchat

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:48.600
go from mere conversation into

00:17:48.600 --> 00:17:50.760
a conspiracy to commit murder.

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:54.150
Well, under 18 U.S.C.
1958, which is the use

00:17:54.150 --> 00:17:55.500
of interstate commerce facilities

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:57.380
in the commission for murder for hire,

00:17:57.380 --> 00:17:59.470
it applies whenever someone travels

00:17:59.470 --> 00:18:02.890
or causes another to travel between states

00:18:02.890 --> 00:18:06.360
and that's sort of a
relic of the federalism

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:07.710
and that a lot of federal statutes

00:18:07.710 --> 00:18:10.270
only apply in interstate situations,

00:18:10.270 --> 00:18:13.400
so that they don't interfere
with state-based laws.

00:18:13.400 --> 00:18:17.870
But, as long as you use the
mail or interstate commerce

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:20.640
with intent that a murder be committed

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:22.880
in violation of the laws
of the United States,

00:18:22.880 --> 00:18:25.510
or if you exchange
anything of pecuniary value

00:18:25.510 --> 00:18:28.610
with anyone who conspires
to commit such an act,

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:31.870
you're guilty of effectively
attempted murder.

00:18:31.870 --> 00:18:35.610
And under 18 U.S.C. 371,
which is the federal statute

00:18:35.610 --> 00:18:38.350
that deals with conspiracy,
all that you need to do

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:43.080
is conduct one overt act in
furtherance of that conspiracy.

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:44.810
And that overt act does not need

00:18:44.810 --> 00:18:47.630
to be one of the elements of that offense.

00:18:47.630 --> 00:18:49.630
So it's a pretty low burden.

00:18:49.630 --> 00:18:52.080
Certainly paying $3000 would definitely

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:54.080
be considered an overt act in furtherance

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:56.050
of a conspiracy to commit murder.

00:18:56.050 --> 00:18:58.110
But I think this documentary is conflating

00:18:58.110 --> 00:18:59.050
a couple different things.

00:18:59.050 --> 00:19:01.400
There are the things that are
required to prove the crime

00:19:01.400 --> 00:19:04.200
and then there are the things
that the U.S. attorneys want

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:06.440
so that they have an easy
time proving the crime

00:19:06.440 --> 00:19:08.867
and those are not
necessarily the same things.

00:19:08.867 --> 00:19:11.200
(suspenseful music)
- When I do become psychotic,

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:13.140
that's my going to Tampa gun.

00:19:13.140 --> 00:19:15.670
- Really just the fact that the guy

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:18.347
started driving toward Carol Baskin

00:19:18.347 --> 00:19:21.110
probably would of been
enough in its own right

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:23.270
to convict both of these people

00:19:23.270 --> 00:19:24.957
of conspiracy to commit murder.

00:19:24.957 --> 00:19:25.807
(suspenseful music)
- I was like, "Hey Joe,

00:19:25.807 --> 00:19:29.887
"this is my guy. This is the
guy who'll whack Carol for ya."

00:19:29.887 --> 00:19:32.390
"Really? How much?"

00:19:32.390 --> 00:19:34.420
- You know, when I said
I was gonna do a reaction

00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:37.130
to "Tiger King", I got so
many comments from people

00:19:37.130 --> 00:19:39.166
that said that Joe was set up

00:19:39.166 --> 00:19:42.430
and that the government
had committed entrapment.

00:19:42.430 --> 00:19:44.610
While entrapment is a defense,

00:19:44.610 --> 00:19:47.180
it's very, very rarely applicable.

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:50.100
And a valid entrapment
defense has two elements.

00:19:50.100 --> 00:19:51.760
One, you have to prove that the government

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:53.440
induced the crime itself.

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:55.900
And, two, you have to
prove the defendant's lack

00:19:55.900 --> 00:19:59.000
of predisposition to engage
in the criminal conduct.

00:19:59.000 --> 00:20:01.860
In other words, that the
government put the idea

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:04.870
of the crime itself into
the defendant's head.

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:07.430
And that's just simply not the case here,

00:20:07.430 --> 00:20:10.180
at least based on what we're
seeing in this documentary.

00:20:10.180 --> 00:20:13.390
I don't think Joe has
any reasonable grounds

00:20:13.390 --> 00:20:15.840
to claim that he was a
victim of entrapment.

00:20:15.840 --> 00:20:18.073
(country music)
♪ Well everything was fine ♪

00:20:18.073 --> 00:20:20.352
♪ Just as sweet as wine ♪

00:20:20.352 --> 00:20:25.352
♪ But her husband went and disappeared ♪

00:20:26.446 --> 00:20:30.108
♪ But then it got a little
crazy, got a little hazy ♪

00:20:30.108 --> 00:20:35.108
♪ And the cop said, "There's
something wrong here" ♪

00:20:35.757 --> 00:20:39.719
♪ Oh here kitty, kitty ♪

00:20:39.719 --> 00:20:42.260
- So the best thing he's done,
it's worth just bringing up,

00:20:42.260 --> 00:20:44.090
the music video "Here Kitty Kitty"

00:20:44.090 --> 00:20:46.280
about Carol killin' her husband.

00:20:46.280 --> 00:20:49.745
- You have to admit that these
songs are a little catchy.

00:20:49.745 --> 00:20:51.860
(chuckles) A lot of
people were asking about

00:20:51.860 --> 00:20:54.110
whether Carol Baskin could
have a defamation claim

00:20:54.110 --> 00:20:57.400
against Joe Exotic, based on
the fact that he's basically

00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:01.110
accusing her of killing her ex-husband.

00:21:01.110 --> 00:21:03.200
Funny story about these songs,

00:21:03.200 --> 00:21:05.690
Joe isn't the one singing in these songs.

00:21:05.690 --> 00:21:08.390
The songs were actually
recorded by two individuals

00:21:08.390 --> 00:21:10.540
named Vince Johnson and Danny Clinton,

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:12.660
who are two musicians
from Washington state.

00:21:12.660 --> 00:21:15.250
Apparently they reached out
to Joe Exotic (chuckles)

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:19.100
and, from my understanding,
they were paid in influence.

00:21:19.100 --> 00:21:20.750
I don't think that Carol Baskin

00:21:20.750 --> 00:21:22.410
could have a defamation claim

00:21:22.410 --> 00:21:24.950
based on the content of these songs.

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:27.320
It's sort of Joe's opinion.

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:29.740
There's some factual basis for it.

00:21:29.740 --> 00:21:32.110
I'm not sure that you
could say he was negligent

00:21:32.110 --> 00:21:34.550
in making this claim,
if it is indeed false,

00:21:34.550 --> 00:21:37.310
and you might argue that Carol Baskin

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:41.180
is a limited purpose
celebrity and therefore

00:21:41.180 --> 00:21:44.240
you would have to use the
higher standard for defamation.

00:21:44.240 --> 00:21:46.675
I don't think that there's
gonna be much of a claim here.

00:21:46.675 --> 00:21:48.410
(suspenseful music)
- And for after just three

00:21:48.410 --> 00:21:50.150
short hours, nearly four hours,

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:53.380
the jury came back finding
Joseph Maldonado-Passage

00:21:53.380 --> 00:21:57.270
guilty on all 19 counts,
two of those largest ones

00:21:57.270 --> 00:21:59.740
for the alleged use of interstate commerce

00:21:59.740 --> 00:22:01.640
for murder for hire.

00:22:01.640 --> 00:22:02.860
- Guilty.

00:22:02.860 --> 00:22:04.920
- I don't know about you but,
after watching "Tiger King",

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:06.710
I had to find out what happened

00:22:06.710 --> 00:22:08.840
to all these people after the show ended.

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:11.440
For better or worse, Joe
Exotic is in federal prison

00:22:11.440 --> 00:22:14.280
in Texas, where he's currently suffering

00:22:14.280 --> 00:22:16.720
from an infection of the coronavirus.

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:18.840
Coronavirus is a really terrible thing

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:21.910
and it's infecting a ton of people in jail

00:22:21.910 --> 00:22:23.480
because they're in close quarters.

00:22:23.480 --> 00:22:25.660
I highly recommend
following Scott Hechinger,

00:22:25.660 --> 00:22:27.760
who's a public defender in Brooklyn.

00:22:27.760 --> 00:22:30.070
He's been at the forefront of advocating

00:22:30.070 --> 00:22:32.280
for prisoners rights and
I think it's definitely

00:22:32.280 --> 00:22:33.490
something you should look into,

00:22:33.490 --> 00:22:35.910
'cause it's not just something
that affected Joe Exotic.

00:22:35.910 --> 00:22:39.500
Joe has filed a lawsuit seeking
nearly 94 million dollars

00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:42.500
in damages against the
Department of Interior,

00:22:42.500 --> 00:22:45.900
the prosecutors and the
documentarians behind the show.

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:48.860
He's going to lose that
suit, I feel very confident.

00:22:48.860 --> 00:22:51.980
The G.W. Zoo is still
open and is being run

00:22:51.980 --> 00:22:53.970
by Jeff Lowe, or at least is was open

00:22:53.970 --> 00:22:57.090
until it was also shut down
for coronavirus reasons.

00:22:57.090 --> 00:22:59.170
As you can imagine, a lot
of people wanna go there

00:22:59.170 --> 00:23:02.410
because of the infamy of this show.

00:23:02.410 --> 00:23:05.210
And because we live in
the dumbest timeline,

00:23:05.210 --> 00:23:07.680
Joe is seeking a presidential pardon

00:23:07.680 --> 00:23:10.850
and President Trump is
actually considering it.

00:23:10.850 --> 00:23:12.170
- I'll take a look.

